---
/**
 * The documentation index.
 *
 * The competing product's docs root is close to empty, so a new visitor lands
 * on nothing and has to guess. This page instead answers "what am I trying to
 * do" first and lists the sections second.
 */
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DOC_SECTIONS, type DocPage } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../data/site';

const page: DocPage = {
  href: '/docs/',
  nav: 'Documentation',
  heading: 'Hozz documentation',
  title: 'Hozz documentation — export Apple Health data to somewhere you own',
  description:
    'Guides for exporting Apple Health data with Hozz: getting started, every destination, every format, the delivery schema, the MCP server, and troubleshooting.',
  lede: 'How to get your Apple Health data out of your phone and into somewhere you control — and what Hozz will and will not promise about it.',
};

/** The three things people actually arrive wanting. */
const paths = [
  {
    href: '/docs/getting-started/',
    tag: 'Start here',
    title: 'I just want my data out',
    body: 'Install, grant Health access, pick a destination, run the first export. Four steps, plus what happens afterwards.',
  },
  {
    href: '/docs/destinations/',
    tag: 'Destinations',
    title: 'I know where I want it to go',
    body: 'A Mac, a folder, Home Assistant, InfluxDB, your own endpoint, or an MQTT broker. One page each.',
  },
  {
    href: '/docs/mcp/',
    tag: 'Assistants',
    title: 'I want to ask questions about it',
    body: 'A read-only MCP server over a database your phone keeps current, so an assistant queries live data rather than a stale XML dump.',
  },
  {
    href: '/docs/delivery-schema/',
    tag: 'Reference',
    title: 'I am building the receiving end',
    body: 'Every field of every delivery format, with a worked example payload and the exact headers.',
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout page={page}>
  <p>
    Today, Hozz reads Apple Health on iPhone and works with the companion Mac app plus existing
    destinations. The phone sends only to destinations <em>you</em> configure. The current app has
    no account, analytics, ads, hosted relay, or default destination.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hozz is <strong>early alpha</strong> and not in the App Store yet. Everything documented here is
    checkable in <a href={GITHUB_URL}>the source</a>; where something is not built, or is built and
    switched off, these pages say so rather than leaving it implied.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>

  <ul class="card-grid card-grid--2">
    {
      paths.map((path) => (
        <li>
          <a class="doc-card" href={path.href}>
            <span class="doc-card-tag">{path.tag}</span>
            <h3>{path.title}</h3>
            <p>{path.body}</p>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>Everything here</h2>

  {
    DOC_SECTIONS.map((section) => (
      <>
        <h3>{section.title}</h3>
        <ul>
          {section.pages.map((entry) => (
            <li>
              <a href={entry.href}>{entry.nav}</a> — {entry.lede}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2>If something here is wrong</h2>

  <p>
    These pages are written against the app's own source, and the lists on them — destinations,
    formats, MCP tools — are checked against it mechanically rather than copied by hand. If you find
    a claim that does not match what the app does, that is a bug in the documentation and worth <a
      href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues`}>raising as an issue</a
    >.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
